46% of Major Web Publishers Use AI Writing 
 ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌ ‌

Hi there, 

Maddie here from Originality.AI!

Today we're going to discuss three very exciting things with you! 

* The heat Sports Illustrated is currently experiencing after being
accused 
of using fake AI Generated authors as well as AI content on their site

* Our in depth study

that shows just how much AI Content has been published on Sport
Illustrated  

* Our study
that
revealed 46% of major publishers were found to rely heavily on AI
writing ( amongst them is The Arena Group, which owns Sports
Illustrated) 

****Sports Illustrated Scandal****

It has been a very exciting week for us in the world of AI Content
Detection! 

This week, Futurism.com
published
a piece called " Sports Illustrated Published Articles by Fake,
AI-Generated Writers- We asked them about it - and they deleted
everything."

For obvious reasons, this kind of investigative journalism (with such a
provoking angle) is our bread and butter, so we jumped at the
opportunity to investigate further. 

Before we dive in, I'll give you a very brief synopsis of the
situation: 

* Drew Ortiz, an outdoors expert and author at Sports Illustrated, was
revealed to be non-existent and generated by AI ( Yikes)

* Concerns were raised about the undisclosed use of AI-generated content
by Sports Illustrated and other publications under The Arena Group.

* Futurism.com's investigation suggests both authors' headshots AND
some articles were created using AI.

* The Arena Group claims the articles were licensed from a third-party
company, AdVon Commerce, both of which denied AI use.

* After some of the initial inquiries, all suspected AI-generated
authors disappear from Sports Illustrated's site (Awkward). 

* Sports Illustrated then shifted blame to the contractor (AdVon
Commerce) for the content saying that Sports Illustrated was assured by
them that all content was human-authored

Sports Illustrated and AdVon's statement says all content was
human-generated:

** **

**"AdVon has assured us that all of the articles in question were
written and edited by humans. According to AdVon, their writers,
editors, and researchers create and curate content and follow a policy
that involves using both counter-plagiarism and counter-AI software on
all content"**

**Some Background on Sports Illustrated and AdVon Commerce
Relationship**

The ArenaGroup (which owns Sports Illustrated) contracted AdVon commerce
to create review articles for a separate section of their site using a
strategy often called "parasite SEO".

"Parasite SEO" is when a company with a reputable domain (such as
Sports Illustrated) partners with a content provider to create articles
("parasites") that can rank well in Google because of the reputable
domain ("host"). 

The quality of the content might be lower than other sites but because
of the authority of the domain, in the eyes of Google the content will
rank well. In the case of the articles at SI.com they made money from
visitors who followed the recommendation of the SI.com article and
purchased a product via an affiliate link.

These review articles were published at si.com/review/ under multiple
fake author names. 

As of June 17, 2022 the SI.com/review site was redirected to the similar
SI.com/showcase section of SI.com. 

https://jonhaver.acemlnb.com/lt.php?s=c2aa77b6f0a29bb9a1c1a13f88329783&i=2096A2241A61A31791 

****Our Sport Illustrated Study and Analysis  ****

So, We know with certainty that there were fake AI Generated authors
used to create reviews, but further questions needed to be answered...

* 

**Was AI used to generate the content?**

* 

**If so- What is the extent of the use of AI?**

We conducted an initial analysis using Originality.AI on an article
identified by Futurism.com as potentially AI-generated. The results were
conclusive, with a 100% probability of AI involvement. 

See scan here: https://jonhaver.acemlnb.com/lt.php?s=c2aa77b6f0a29bb9a1c1a13f88329783&i=2096A2241A61A31791

Because Sports Illustrated deleted a lot of their content after the
accusations started flying, below is the method we used to create a
dataset for a larger and more robust analysis of Sports Illustrated's
content investigation:  

Two sites we investigated: 

* 

**SI.com/Review (redirected as of June 17, 2022)**

* 

**SI.com/Showcase**

****Results****

**Originality.ai/AI-Checker**  was
Used to Analyze 3 different parts of each of the 261 review articles at 

**SI.com/Review** which were still accessible via Archive.org. AI
writing was suspected on many of the articles...

* Introduction: 68.5% of Articles suspected of being AI written 

* Review Section: 34.5% of Articles suspected of being AI written

* Article Body: 36.6% of Articles suspected of being AI written

Originality.ai/AI-Checker  was used
to analyze 3 different parts of each of the 986 review articles at 

**SI.com/Showcase** available. 

* Introduction: 48.9% of Articles suspected of being AI written

* Review Section: 3.7% of Articles suspected of being AI written

* Article Body: 23.5% of Articles suspected of being AI written

I think the BIG finding from this study is that there is minimal/no sign
of AI being used to write the product "review" section of each
article. SI.com and their contracted 3rd party editorial team seem to
have effectively implemented controls to ensure at least the product
review portion of the articles are written by humans. 

See Full Sports Illustrated Study

**The Bigger Picture: Sector-Wide AI Writing Trends**

Our research extends beyond Sports Illustrated to encompass 16 major
companies dominating Google search results. 

We wanted to know how many of these publishers rely on AI Generated
content. Startling statistics have emerged, revealing that 46% of major
publishers are actively incorporating AI into their content creation
processes!

****Key Findings:***** 7 of the 16 companies' have websites with
significant amounts of AI content 

* Companies that had the most AI content on their sites were:

* Valnet

* The Arena Group (which owns Sports Illustrated)

* Red Ventures

* DotDashMeredith

* Internet Brands

* VoxMedia

* Ziff Davis.

* 76 (46.1%) of the 165 total websites analyzed have significant AI
content 

See Full Study

Our analysis and studies reflect a broader trend in the media industry,
where publishers use AI tools for content creation, often
without  disclosing it, leading to potential quality, transparency and
ethical concerns.

What are your thoughts on these issues? We would love your feedback! 

Sent to: patriogabrielpatdu@gmail.com

